6. Foundation Principles

Every Atlas specification SHALL satisfy the following principles.

6.1 Consistency

Shared technical concepts SHALL be defined once within AFS.

Other specifications SHALL reference AFS rather than redefining common concepts.

6.2 Modularity

Every specification family SHALL remain independently maintainable.

Shared infrastructure SHALL reside within AFS.

6.3 Separation of Concerns

AFS SHALL define infrastructure.

Domain specifications SHALL define domain behavior.

Infrastructure SHALL NOT contain engineering semantics.

6.4 Technology Independence

AFS SHALL remain independent of:

Programming languages
Databases
Workflow engines
Cloud providers
Vendors
Operating systems
6.5 Interoperability

Every Atlas specification SHALL interoperate through common foundation mechanisms defined by AFS.

6.6 Extensibility

Future Atlas specifications SHALL extend the framework without modifying existing foundation semantics.

6.7 Stability

AFS SHALL evolve conservatively.

Changes affecting multiple specification families SHALL require architectural review.

6.8 Backward Compatibility

Foundation changes SHOULD preserve compatibility wherever technically feasible.

Breaking changes SHALL require a new major version.

7. Shared Foundation Services

The Atlas Foundation Specification defines the shared infrastructure for:

